# [[.Title]]

A Steward plugin for [[.Title]], built with [`@treno-dev/steward-sdk`](https://github.com/treno-dev/steward-sdk/tree/main/js).

## Run

```sh
npm install
steward plugin validate node src/plugin.js
```

A plugin is started by a runner, not by hand: run directly it says so and exits. Use
`steward plugin validate` to check it, and `steward plugin run --context context.json node src/plugin.js`
to invoke it. Write logs to standard error.

## Test

Save this as `context.json`, the config and resources to call the plugin with:

```json
{
  "config": {
    "inputs": { "base_url": "https://example.test" },
    "secrets": { "API_TOKEN": "secret" }
  },
  "identity": { "external_id": "someone@example.test" },
  "role": { "name": "viewer", "permissions": [{ "name": "view" }] },
  "resources": [{ "kind": "item", "name": "demo", "inputs": { "description": "A demo item" } }]
}
```

```sh
steward plugin run --context context.json node src/plugin.js
steward plugin run --context context.json --call provision node src/plugin.js
```

The first creates each resource, grants and revokes the role, and removes it. It does this for real, in
whatever your credentials reach, and asks before it starts (`--yes` skips the question). `--call` makes
only the calls you name, once each and without cleaning up: `provision`, `grant-access`, `get-access`,
`revoke-access` and `deprovision`. `steward plugin validate` never changes anything.

## Where things are

`src/plugin.js` declares the plugin's inputs (what it needs to connect), each kind of resource and the
handlers Steward calls: `provision`, `deprovision`, `grantAccess`, `revokeAccess` and `getAccess`.
Delete the handlers you do not need: Steward offers only what you implement.

To add a kind of resource, call `plugin.resource({ kind, ... })` again with its own inputs, outputs and
handlers.

See the [SDK README](https://github.com/treno-dev/steward-sdk/blob/main/js/README.md) for
everything a plugin can declare.
